<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mogilev</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mahilyow</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mogilev, or Mahilyow, is a city in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belarus">Belarus</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>, it grew into an important trading town and received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> rights in 1577. It passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-partition-of-poland">First Partition of Poland</a> in 1772, and in 1915-1917 it was the headquarters of the Russian army.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mogilev.jpg" alt="Mogilev" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Liashko · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahilio%C5%AD,_ratu%C5%A1a._%D0%9C%D0%B0%D0%B3%D1%96%D0%BB%D1%91%D1%9E,_%D1%80%D0%B0%D1%82%D1%83%D1%88%D0%B0_(2020).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
